          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How long does a common cold usually last?",
          "answer": "A common cold usually runs its course in about **7 to 10 days**. The antibiotic stewardship guidance says a cold lasting this long is running its normal course. A cough can linger **two to three weeks** after the cold has gone, and this is also expected. (Source: *Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated*.)\n\nYellow or green nasal discharge does not by itself mean a bacterial infection. It is part of the normal viral course. (Same source.)\n\n**When to seek care:** The clinical reference on upper respiratory infection (common cold) says further assessment is advised when symptoms get worse after an early improvement, or when fever returns. Its full list was cut off in what I retrieved, so please check with a clinician or pharmacist if you are unsure.\n\n**Seek emergency care straight away** if a cold or any infection comes with any of these, which are described in the emergency guidance on sepsis and meningitis:\n- Confusion, unusual drowsiness, or difficulty waking\n- Very fast or difficult breathing\n- A rash of small red or purple spots that does not fade when pressed\n- A severe headache with a stiff neck, or a strong dislike of bright light\n- Fits (seizures)\n- Not passing urine for many hours, or feeling much worse than you ever have before\n\nThis is general information, not a diagnosis. If you are worried about your own symptoms, speak to a doctor, nurse, or pharmacist.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
